nyra wrote on 09/21/2003 08:14 PM:
[...]

> There might be some eastern myth of privileged people changing into a
> whole new adult body[1], but in the western re-incarnation schools,
> having to go through the troubles of being born is part of the severe
> punishment physical existence and re-incarnation represent.

There's no need to follow one myth above the other. Having not found the
Amulet you deserve to be reincarnated, having been chosen for the quest
indicates you might deserve an adult body. The question is why should
the body where you reincarnate to be just that _same_ body you were
before. Perhaps the "oRei has some metaphysical properties which make it
serve as a guiding light for the souls to find their former bodies. They
might be freed of some of their spirit's earthly posessions such as
memories, etc. but they will find the body and (what is much more
important in NetHack's meterial world) -- the equipment.

> [1] i suspect that this might be reserved to persons of practically
> divine status, though; i've just finished reading something on the
> Cathars (a christian sect which believed in reincarnation) and _they_
> believed that Jesus was the only human-shaped being who escaped the
> humiliation of being born by so vile a thing ("in re tam vili") as a
> woman (the material world was entirely evil to them, and the female
> body was the tool by which children came into this world - the
> ultimate tool of evil).

Then cathar myth seems entirely inappropriate for NetHack. After all,
the most important thing there, our reason for existence and divinie
inspiration, is a material object.

>> If your deity is angry with you, have bad luck or both then you may be
>> reincarnated at a lower experience level or you may not be reincarnated
>> at all.
> 
> Going by the common reincarnation beliefs, not being reincarnated is
> the ultimate *reward* everyone should be striving for. I guess you
> might _not_ be re-incarnated if you died a "good" death while being
> the crowned champion of your alignment (you'd go straight to Nirvana
> or the lost Paradise); in every other case, you _would_ re-incarnate,
> but you'd lose all your memories.

Good ideas, except that w/o having found the Amulet (of Yendor) you
shouldn't break the cycle--you were CHOSEN for the quest after all, and
your spirit will not be eligible before you complete it. Perhaps, in the
very early stages, there should be a chance that you don't get
reincarnated (perhaps depending on Luck; NOT depending on $deity
relationship),

Another idea would be to exchange the "oLS with an "oRei for the classes
where it makes sence (Mon, Sam, probably Val?) and leave the "oLS
functions as before for the other ones.

